			"66": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 2: EXPANDING TO SIZE\n\nIn this example we have a Grid Pattern node sending a Float2 collection to the Transform node which in turn puts the Rectangles on the right spot.\nAt the bottom we have two Linear nodes generating collections of either 0.5 or 1.\nThe size of the linear gets updated when the Grid Pattern changes size, for this we use the Size node.\nNext, the collections are joined together using the Join node and send to the scale inlet of the Transform node.\nWe end up with a collection that is twice the size of the Grid pattern, because of this Wire will change the instance count of  the Transform node to the largest collection and repeat smaller collections (the grid pattern in this case).\n\nNow we have two sets of rectangles laying on top of each other in a grid pattern.\nThe first set of rectangles has a scale of 0.5, the second set has a scale of 1.\nWe can visualize this by applying the Edge node and we see that we have rectangles inside of rectangles.\n\nNext we want to apply color to the rectangles.\nWe take the first color and use the Expand node to create a collection of colors equal to the size of the Grid Pattern.\nWe do this again for the second color and Join them together.\nWhen we apply this to the Shape Render we can see that the first color is applied to the inner rectangle(scale = 0.5) and the second color is applied to the outer rectangle(scale = 1)."
					}
				},
